<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Detmold</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Detmold is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. It became the main residence of the rulers of Lippe, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palace still stands, and was the capital of the Principality and then the Free State of Lippe until 1947. The Hermannsdenkmal monument and the Externsteine rocks lie nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/detmold.jpg" alt="Detmold" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikater · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detmold_Schloss01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Detmold is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> and today the administrative centre of the district of Lippe and of the Regierungsbezirk Detmold. About 5 kilometres to its south-west lies the Grotenburg hill, with a prehistoric circular rampart and the Hermann monument. The monument commemorates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> of 9 CE, in which Germanic tribes led by Hermann, in Latin Arminius, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>, though the battle may or may not have been fought near the present town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Detmold was first mentioned as Theotmalli in 783, the year of a battle between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s forces nearby, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a>. In 1263 Bernard III of Lippe fortified the settlement at the crossing of the trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemgo">Lemgo</a> over the Werre with stone walls and gave it a municipal charter. Market rights granted in 1265 led to rapid economic growth. Its population was reported as 305 in 1305. Its defences were strengthened after severe damage in the conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soest">Soest</a> in 1447, and a fire in 1547 destroyed more than 70 houses.</p><p>In 1550 Detmold became the permanent residence of Count Simon III of Lippe. The counts became princes in 1789, and the town remained capital of the small Principality of Lippe until 1918, when all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> were abolished. From 1919 to 1947 it was the capital of the Free State of Lippe. When Lippe was incorporated into the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, the town became the seat of the Lippe district.</p><p>Street lighting with oil lanterns was introduced in 1809. By 1835 Detmold was the most populous town in Lippe, with over 4,000 residents, and it grew to 12,000 in 1900 and over 30,000 in 1950. In the administrative reform of 1970, 25 nearby villages were incorporated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle stands in the town park. Other sights include the Externsteine, the Hermannsdenkmal, the Lippisches Landesmuseum and the Detmold open-air museum. The city is known for its Music Academy and the Landestheater Detmold, and the Church of Lippe has its central administration there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> lived in Detmold in the winters of 1857–60. The population is 74,438.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Detmold” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Leopold IV abdicated on 12 November 1918, and Lippe became a free state with <b>Detmold</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-lippe">Free State of Lippe</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Lippe held lands around <b>Detmold</b> and Lemgo from the 12th century, becoming counts in 1528.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-lippe">Principality of Lippe</a></li></ul></section>
